hls: a segment in hand is read under its own number

A reader of a live playlist falls behind it when whoever reads from the
reader stops for a while: a player that is paused, or one whose queues
are full. It then finds the segments it wanted gone from the list, and
goes on at the first one the list still has, "skipping N segments
ahead, expired from playlists". Between two segments that is as it
should be.

It also happened in the middle of one. With a connection kept for the
next request, input_read_done says that the input in hand has been read
to its end. Since the reading was divided into reload_playlist() and
read_data_continuous(), the flag is no longer cleared where the next
segment is opened but at the top of the call after that one, and before
it is cleared that call looks at the playlist as if a segment were about
to be opened. So the playlist was read again at the second read of
every segment. If the list had by then moved on past the segment being
read, that segment was counted as the one the list now began with, and
the one after that was asked for next: a segment that was on the list
was never read, and every byte range, key and discontinuity from there
on was looked up under a number one too high for the bytes in hand.

A viewer who paused a live channel for longer than its list reaches
back, and played on, lost segments that were still listed, one each
time the reader came to the end of a segment that had left the list.
The smaller the segments, the surer: one of 32 kB or less is in hand
whole at its second read.

The flag is cleared where the segment's input is put in place, as it
was before the division, and the playlist is read again only between
segments.

Between segments there was a second way to the same end. Where the
server speaks HTTP/1.1, the segment after the one being read is asked
for ahead of time, over a second connection. When the list is then
found to have moved on, that answer was still taken as the next input,
and read as the list's first segment. It is let go of, as is the
connection kept open to read on in the same file, which the list's
first segment need not be in.

--- a/libavformat/hls.c
+++ b/libavformat/hls.c
@@ -1800,6 +1800,16 @@
                    "skipping %"PRId64" segments ahead, expired from playlists\n",
                    v->start_seq_no - v->cur_seq_no);
             v->cur_seq_no = v->start_seq_no;
+            /* What was got ready for the segment after the last one is
+             * not for the one the list begins with: the answer to a
+             * request made ahead of time would be read as that segment,
+             * and a connection kept to read on in the same file may be
+             * to another file. */
+            if (v->input_next_requested) {
+                ff_format_io_close(v->parent, &v->input_next);
+                v->input_next_requested = 0;
+            }
+            v->input_reuse = 0;
         }
         if (v->cur_seq_no > v->last_seq_no) {
             v->last_seq_no = v->cur_seq_no;
@@ -1837,14 +1847,6 @@
     int segment_retries = 0;
     struct segment *seg;
 
-    if (c->http_persistent && v->input_read_done) {
-        ret = reload_playlist(v, c);
-        if (ret < 0)
-            return ret;
-    }
-
-    v->input_read_done = 0;
-
 restart:
     ret = reload_playlist(v, c);
     if (ret < 0)
@@ -1885,6 +1887,11 @@
         }
         segment_retries = 0;
         just_opened = 1;
+        /* The input now in hand is this segment's, with all of it still
+         * to be read. Until it has been, the playlist is left alone:
+         * read again, it may have moved on past this segment, which
+         * would then be counted as the one the list begins with. */
+        v->input_read_done = 0;
     }
 
     if (c->http_multiple == -1) {
